  {
   "id": "p2-6",
   "name": "Calculating distance, time and speed"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The formula v = d/t links speed (v), distance (d) and time (t).\n• Speed: v = d ÷ t.\n• Distance: d = v × t.\n• Time: t = d ÷ v.\nA triangle helps: d on top, v and t at the bottom. Cover the one you want.\nUse matching units: m and s give m/s; km and h give km/h. Convert first if they do not match (for example minutes to hours).\nExample: a bus at 60 km/h for 3 h travels 60 × 3 = 180 km. To travel 150 km at 50 km/h takes 150 ÷ 50 = 3 h.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "v": 60,
   "t": 30,
   "sixty": 60
  },
  "problem": "A taxi travels at {v} km/h for {t} minutes. How far does it go?",
  "steps": [
   "Units must match: {t} minutes = {t} ÷ {sixty} = {t / sixty} h.",
   "d = v × t = {v} × {t / sixty} = {v * t / sixty} km."
  ],
  "answer": "{v * t / sixty} km",
  "checks": [
   "v * t / sixty == 30"
  ]
 },
 "teach": {
